{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "MosaicMesaSaltEquilibrium",
    "description": "MOSAIC's MESA solid-liquid equilibrium (Zaveri, Easter and Wexler, 2005; WRF v4.8.0 chem/module_mosaic_therm.F, SUBROUTINE mesa_ptc with mesa_flux_salt and mesa_convergence_criterion), for ONE size bin, written as WHAT IS SOLVED rather than how mesa_ptc solves it.\n\nTHE PROBLEM. The unknown is the solid electrolyte vector E_s (the fifteen soluble salts; CaSO4 and CaCO3 are always solid and come with the state). Mass balance gives the liquid species as the totals less the ions of the solid (electrolytes_to_ions(jsolid)): a_l = a_t - sum_e E_s,e nu_e (`a_l_state`). From that liquid, ions_to_electrolytes.esm forms the liquid electrolytes (any CaSO4 it finds is insoluble and joins the solid, `caso4`), aerosol_water_zsr.esm gives the water and mtem_activities.esm the activities a_s; this component then gives each salt's saturation ratio S_s = a_s/K_s(T) (`sat`, K_s = keq_sl by template fn_keq) and the pseudo-time rate\n\n    dE_s/dtau = S_s - 1   (`flux`),\n\nswitched off (rate 0) for a salt that is undersaturated with no solid to dissolve (the complementarity of E_s >= 0), for Ca(NO3)2 and CaCl2 always (MOSAIC never lets them precipitate), and, when calcium is dissolved, for a salt under 1% of the liquid electrolytes or not present in the totals. The equilibrium MESA seeks is the steady state of that relaxation: every salt either has solid and S_s = 1, or has none and S_s <= 1, i.e. flux_s = 0 for every s with E_s >= 0. `phi` = (S_s - 1)/max(S_s, 1) is the same residual scaled to [-1, 1) -- the quantity MOSAIC's 1% tolerance is stated in. pseudo-time tau carries amount units (nmol/m3), as in the Fortran, where a step h_s times the dimensionless flux is an amount.\n\nTHE RESIDUAL A SOLVER IS GIVEN (`g`). `flux` has the complementarity written into it (zero for an undersaturated salt with no solid), which is how mesa_flux_salt forms it; a bounded EarthSciIntegrator `solve` (D95) supplies that complementarity itself from the bound E_s >= 0, so it is handed `g` = S_s - 1 with only MOSAIC's own switch-offs (`model_off`: Ca(NO3)2, CaCl2, and the two calcium conditions; and no water). The equilibrium is E_s >= 0, g_s <= 0, E_s g_s = 0, equivalently flux_s = 0 for every salt. couplings/mosaic_mesa_equilibrium.esm states it as that problem (E_s algebraic unknowns, `0 ~ g`) with its run configuration beside it.\n\nWHICH SWITCH-OFFS ARE PHYSICS. The three Heaviside conditions change WHICH state is the equilibrium (a supersaturated salt that MOSAIC holds liquid because calcium is dissolved is part of MOSAIC's model, not of its solver), so they stay. mesa_dissolve_small_salt -- an undersaturated salt under 1% of the solid is returned to the liquid outright -- does not: at an equilibrium an undersaturated salt has no solid anyway, so the snap only shortens the path and is not written here (the rate there is S_s - 1 < 0, which drives the same salt to zero). The 98% rule (`all_solid`) and the all-liquid test (`all_liquid`) are MOSAIC's PHASE CLASSIFICATION of a state -- downstream, an all-solid bin loses its water (adjust_solid_aerosol) -- so they are kept as functions of the state; in mesa_ptc they are also exits of the iteration.\n\nWHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY (CLAUDE.md: .esm files never implement a numerical solver). mesa_ptc integrates the relaxation by explicit Euler with a separate adaptive step per salt -- h_s = alpha_s min(tau_p, tau_d), with alpha_s from the history of |phi| (phi_bar, caps 0.9/0.5, floor -10) and tau_p from mesa_estimate_eleliquid's estimate of how much of the salt the liquid holds -- and stops when every |phi_s| <= rtol_mesa = 0.01 or after nmax_mesa = 80 passes. None of that (the step fraction, the estimate, the time scales, the update E_s <- E_s + h_s flux_s, the iteration counter, the 1% test, the 80-pass cap, the clipping of negative solids on exit) is physics; it belongs to EarthSciIntegrator, which solves the problem above (a `solve` node, see INVENTORY.md and data/eqwefic/notes/mosaic_mesa_kelvin_solvers.md). FORTRAN_BUGS B30 (mesa_estimate_eleliquid reads an uninitialised H+) therefore lives only in the step size: it changes mesa_ptc's path and stopping point, never the equilibrium.\n\nFORTRAN NOTES. With no liquid water mesa_flux_salt zeroes every flux and returns (`wet`); phi is then left at the previous pass's value, a solver artefact written here as 0. Na3H(SO4)2's activity is set to zero before S is formed, so it never precipitates (sat 0).\n\nPRECISION. Double precision with MOSAIC's default-REAL literals at their binary32 values (mosaic_mesa_parameters.esm). electrolytes_to_ions is written as the stoichiometric sum over electrolytes, not in the Fortran's own order of terms; the two agree within the tests' tolerance, including the liquid, total minus solid, which can be a small difference (an absolute floor of 1e-12 of the bin's largest total amount).\n\nTESTS (option 1 of the 2026-11-12 decision): the rate and residual evaluated INSTANTANEOUSLY at states mesa_ptc actually visited, from the MESA tape (WRF fork earthsciml-instrumented-chem170 module_esm_mesa_tape at every pass, kernels/mosaic_therm_driver; data/eqwefic/chem170_scm/mesa64): the column hand-offs of the chem170 aerosol_phase_state capture replayed bit for bit, plus hand-specified bins for the branches the column never reaches. Inputs: the pass's starting solid (TOP record) and its ions_to_electrolytes / water / activity results (THERM); expected: S, phi, flux and the liquid fractions (FLUX), the mass balance against the TOP record's own solid and liquid ions, keq_sl / jsalt_present / dry masses (ENTRY), and the phase flags. Where mesa_ptc snapped a small undersaturated salt on that pass its flux_sl is 0 by the snap; the rate asserted there is S - 1 (the taped S), and the solid-mass flags of that pass are not asserted (the Fortran forms them after the snap). Real64 at rel 1e-9, flags exactly. The Fortran's STOPPING POINT is not asserted: how far it is from the equilibrium is measured in data/eqwefic/notes/mosaic_mesa_kelvin_solvers.md. Tests are chosen by a census of physics branches (tools/fill_mosaic_mesa_tests.py): 29 visited states; plus 12 CONVERGED states (mesa_ptc rerun to rtol 1e-10 on column hand-offs, 6 of which its own 80-pass cap had left unconverged), where the rate must be zero and g in complementarity with E_s, at an absolute 2e-10 (twice that stopping test); couplings/mosaic_mesa_salt_rates.esm evaluates the same 29 states from the solid alone (1969). Mutation-checked 2026-11-12 after the split into model_off / g (failing assertions over this file and couplings/mosaic_mesa_salt_rates.esm): g with the complementarity written in (832), g without model_off (294), flux without the complementarity (835), model_off without the calcium-presence condition (20), model_off without Ca(NO3)2/CaCl2 (152) -- the last two newly caught: the five-way `or` they used to sit in evaluated only its first two arguments on the be1fafe6d binary, so both were dead; it is now nested. Earlier mutation checks: the rate S rather than S - 1 (35), no complementarity switch-off (557), no calcium small-fraction switch-off (2), no calcium presence switch-off (2), phi unscaled (19), the mass balance's sign (40), the 98% all-solid fraction at 0.97 (1). Not caught, because no visited state reaches them: Na3H(SO4)2's zeroed activity kept (no visited bin has a nonzero MTEM activity for it), this state's CaSO4 left out of the solid (ions_to_electrolytes never finds new CaSO4 inside mesa_ptc), and the 30% crustal threshold at 20% (no state lies between). Of the asserted rates, those that differ from the Fortran's flux_sl are exactly the salts mesa_dissolve_small_salt snapped on that pass -- the only place the removed solver heuristics touch a rate.\n\nCELL AXES (2026-11-12). Every quantity of the bin carries the cell axes [lev, bin] ahead of its own axes, and every quantity of the grid cell (t_k, keq_sl) carries [lev] (metaparameters NLEV and NBIN, both 1 by default), so a coupling evaluates every size section of every grid cell at once by binding NLEV and NBIN at the mount. The equations are the single-bin ones: an elementwise relation is written on bare names, whose operands esm-spec 4.3.4 aligns by index-set name, and only the reductions and gathers name the cell subscripts k (level) and b (section). The axes are the component's own because pointwise lifting, which would let a single-bin component be mounted per bin, applies to 0-D systems only (esm-spec 10.5).",
    "authors": [
      "EqWeFiC"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R.A., Easter, R.C., Peters, L.K. (2005). A computationally efficient Multicomponent Equilibrium Solver for Aerosols (MESA). J. Geophys. Res. 110, D24203."
      },
      {
        "citation": "WRF v4.8.0 chem/module_mosaic_therm.F"
      }
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NLEV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 1,
      "description": "Grid cells (model levels) evaluated at once; 1 for a single cell. Bound by a column coupling."
    },
    "NBIN": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 1,
      "description": "MOSAIC size sections per cell (nbin_a); 1 for a single bin. Bound by the coupling that mounts this component (8 in chem_opt = 170)."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "lev": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NLEV"
    },
    "bin": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NBIN"
    },
    "salt": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 15
    },
    "sp": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 7
    },
    "el": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 22
    },
    "ion": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 9
    },
    "aer": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 112
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "MosaicMesaSaltEquilibrium": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "./mosaic_thermo_templates.esm"
        }
      ],
      "subsystems": {
